    "product_id": "78d666ff-95ce-424e-b9e5-5f2250182cf1",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 221100\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n400 AM MST Sat Aug 22 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- High temperatures reach or exceed 110 degrees nearly every day \nthrough the next week and will be within a few degrees of daily \nrecords, while morning lows remain unusually warm. \n\n- Extreme Heat Warnings are in effect through Friday evening with \ndaily major to extreme HeatRisk.\n\n- Thunderstorms will primarily favor higher terrain areas through \nearly next week, with drier and less favorable conditions next week.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nExtreme heat will be the primary weather hazard through the short \nand long term forecast. Early morning objective analysis shows a \n596dm high pressure centered over CO/NM. Models show that this high \nwill slowly progress eastward through the beginning of next week. \nDespite the center of the high moving away from our region, H5 \nheights over our region will remain in a 594-597dm range. With \nheights of this magnitude, the NBM continues to show afternoon highs \n(morning lows) of 110-116 (83-91) degrees across the lower deserts \nand 100-110 (70-80) degrees across the higher terrain areas. \nTemperatures of this magnitude are 5-8 degrees above daily normals \nand some locations could tie or break their daily records (see \nClimate section below). These temperatures will lead to widespread \nhigh-end moderate to major HeatRisk across much of the CWA and \ntherefore the Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect.\n\nThe best thunderstorm chances will exist across the higher terrain \nareas through the beginning of next week with only slight chances \nfor some isolated activity across the lower deserts. Thunderstorm \nchances across the lower deserts for this afternoon are around 10-\n20%. While some hi-res models (HRRR and RRFS) are trying to pick up \non some more scattered activity across south-central AZ (including \nthe Phoenix Metro), they are likely overdoing the coverage. Models \nshow limited moisture today (PWATs of 1.0-1.3\"), mixing ratios only \naround 6-7 g/kg, MUCAPE values less then 500 J/kg, and around 25-50 \nJ/kg of CIN. The hi-res models showing scattered to widespread \nactivity for this evening are likely picking up on some upper-level \ndivergence and colliding outflows. While there will likely be \nmultiple outflows moving off the higher terrain this evening not \nmuch activity (other than some isolated showers and maybe a few \nisolated storms) is expected even if they do collide due to all the \nlimiting factors mentioned earlier. Strong to locally severe wind \ngusts and areas of blowing dust will be the primary hazard with any \nstorm this afternoon and evening, with high DCAPE values (1800-2000 \nJ/kg). \n\nModels are in decent agreement that there will be a slight increase \nin moisture on Sunday and Monday (PWATs increase to around 1.1-1.6\" \nacross the area) with the highest moisture expected on Monday. \nInstability will be about the same on Sunday as today, but will \nincrease to around 1000-1500 J/kg on Monday. Additionally, mixing \nrations will increase to around 10 g/kg on Monday. Despite the \nincrease in moisture, instability, and mixing ratios models show an \nincrease in CIN (100-200 J/kg). Activity each day will depend on how \nthe day prior plays out. But right now, Monday looks to have \nslightly better chances than Sunday. Either way, most of the lower \ndeserts are expected to remain dry with the best chances (and \ncoverage) of thunderstorms expected across the higher terrain areas \nto the east of the Phoenix Metro. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /TUESDAY THROUGH NEXT FRIDAY/... \nEnsemble members are in good agreement that by Tuesday drier air \nwill start to filter back into the region as the subtropical high \nstarts to retrograde back over our region. By the middle of the week \nthe center of the high will be straddling the AZ/NM border and by \nthe end of the week the center of the high will be over AZ. As the \nhigh moves overhead, PWATs will drop into a 0.8-1.2\" range across \nour region and steering flow will be out of the S/SW. The \ncombination of these factors will lead to poor chances for \nthunderstorm development across the region. The NBM shows that \nTuesday and Wednesday there could be enough moisture around to \nsupport isolated thunderstorm chances for the higher terrain areas. \nBut by the end of next week thunderstorm chances for our area shut \noff and will mainly be relegated to the Mogollon Rim. \n\nUnusually hot temperatures will be the main story next week. As \nmentioned above the subtropical high will slowly retrograde back \nover our region beginning on Tuesday, which will result in a gradual \nwarming trend for our area. The high is also expected to strengthen \nto around 596-598dam during this time. The combination of the center \nsettling over our region and strengthening will result in \ntemperatures heating up to around 10 degrees above normal for this \ntime of year. Afternoon highs (morning lows) are forecasted to be in \nthe 110-116 (85-95) degree range across the lower deserts and in the \n100-110 (75-85) degree range across the higher terrain areas. The \nincrease in temperatures will also result in an increase in \nHeatRisk, with widespread extreme HeatRisk forecast for the majority \nof the CWA by Wednesday. There remains high confidence in the heat \nforecast for next week. So, the Extreme Heat Warning remains in \neffect through next Friday and will likely need to be expanded to \nmore areas by the middle of the week and may eventually need to be \nextended into next weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1100Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nUncertainty in TSRA/SHRA coverage and corresponding gusty outflow \nwinds/wind shifts will be the primary forecast challenge through \nSunday morning under periods of mid/high cloud decks. Confidence is \ngood that light east winds will prevail through early/mid afternoon \nwith a slow transition to W/SW by late afternoon. TS development \nover higher terrain areas may send gusty outflow winds towards the \nPhoenix terminals during the early evening with a S/SE direction \nmost likely. While direct TS impacts are not likely, briefly gusty \nwinds and decaying SHRA could affect operations mid evening. If more \nconcentrated storms can materialize along with multiple colliding \noutflow boundaries, better chances for isolated TS development in \nlower elevations will be enhanced with stronger and more prolonged \nwind gusts. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo major weather issues will exist through Sunday morning under \nmostly clear skies. Trends in winds should be similar to the past 24 \nhours, generally varying between SE and SW with extended periods of \nnearly calm conditions in between. TS activity over the Baja spine \nlate this afternoon may send an outflow and decaying SHRA towards \nKIPL prior to sunset. Chances for direct TSRA impacts are too low to \ninclude in this TAF package, however gusty SW winds justify a \ncontinued mention through the early evening. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nHot and seasonally dry conditions are expected nearly every day, \nthrough next week. Aside from a brief increase in moisture Sunday-\nMonday, minimum RH values each afternoon will be around 12-18% (15-\n25% Sun-Mon). Temperatures will be around 5-10 degrees above normal \nthrough the next week, with lower desert highs eclipsing 110 degrees \nmost afternoons. There will be slight chances for storms each \nafternoon through Monday, but they will mostly favor the higher \nterrain areas. Drier conditions, with lower storm chances are \nexpected by the middle of next week. Outside of thunderstorm outflow \nwinds, winds will follow the typical diurnal tendencies with some \nmodest afternoon upslope gustiness.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...\nRecord highs:\n\nDate       Phoenix         Yuma         El Centro\n----       -------         ----         ---------\nAug 22   113 in 2011    115 in 1969    117 in 1969\nAug 23   114 in 2011    115 in 2011    116 in 1969\nAug 24   115 in 2020    119 in 1985    117 in 1985\nAug 25   113 in 2011    116 in 1962    117 in 1962\nAug 26   117 in 2011    115 in 2020    115 in 1981\nAug 27   114 in 2023    120 in 1981    117 in 1981\nAug 28   117 in 2023    118 in 2023    117 in 2009\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ530>555-559-\n     560.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Friday for CAZ562-565>567-\n     569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Berislavich\nLONG TERM...Berislavich \nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Berislavich/Benedict \nCLIMATE...18\n",
